WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.660
Two children can both have a cerebral cortex that formed differently, for opposite

2
00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:13.220
reasons. In one child, the cortex is small, but its layers sit in the usual order.

3
00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:18.520
In the other, the cortex is thick and nearly smooth, and many neurons sit in the

4
00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:21.860
wrong place. Which step of building the cortex went wrong?

5
00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:24.470
Building a cortex is two jobs.

6
00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:27.970
First, radial glia line the fluid -filled ventricle.

7
00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:31.370
They divide to make more of themselves, and then to make neurons.

8
00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:36.890
Second, each new neuron moves outward along a radial glial fiber to its layer.

9
00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:40.650
So there are two broad ways to go wrong, and they can overlap.

10
00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:42.550
Too few neurons are made.

11
00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:45.130
Or neurons are made, but they stall on the way.

12
00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:50.260
Take the first. Early on, progenitors divide in a symmetric way.

13
00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:54.860
Each division makes two progenitors, so each round doubles the pool.

14
00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.260
Later they switch to making neurons.

15
00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:02.360
In this simple model, a switch one round early halves the pool, and so halves the

16
00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:07.180
neurons. Primary microcephaly usually leaves the layers broadly normal.

17
00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:11.000
A leading idea says its genes act through this timing.

18
00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:17.080
Most of them, such as ASPM, encode proteins at the spindle pole of a dividing cell.

19
00:01:17.883 --> 00:01:19.923
A virus can strike the same step.

20
00:01:20.763 --> 00:01:25.983
Zika virus infected human cortical progenitors, grown from stem cells, far more

21
00:01:25.983 --> 00:01:28.743
readily than stem cells or immature neurons.

22
00:01:29.303 --> 00:01:32.443
It killed some progenitors and slowed the growth of the rest.

23
00:01:33.163 --> 00:01:38.623
Two cautions. This work used cultured cells and organoids, so it shows what the

24
00:01:38.623 --> 00:01:43.563
virus can do. And most infections in pregnancy do not produce microcephaly.

25
00:01:44.103 --> 00:01:47.243
Still, the virus attacks the cells that make neurons.

26
00:01:47.863 --> 00:01:52.903
So the cortex can be short of neurons, and tissue already built can be damaged too.

27
00:01:53.667 --> 00:01:55.487
Now the second, lissencephaly.

28
00:01:56.087 --> 00:01:58.407
Here the progenitors were not the main problem.

29
00:01:58.967 --> 00:02:00.307
The neurons failed to travel.

30
00:02:00.907 --> 00:02:06.587
When LIS1 was knocked down in rat embryos, progenitor divisions also stopped, and

31
00:02:06.587 --> 00:02:09.047
most new cells stayed many branched.

32
00:02:09.407 --> 00:02:14.367
In the few that moved, neurons kept extending their leading process, but the nucleus

33
00:02:14.367 --> 00:02:19.307
barely moved. Another gene, DCX, makes double cordon.

34
00:02:19.807 --> 00:02:23.827
It binds microtubules and stabilizes them in migrating neurons.

35
00:02:24.647 --> 00:02:29.967
A male has 1X. If it carries the mutation, no neuron makes working double cordon,

36
00:02:30.167 --> 00:02:31.467
and most neurons stall.

37
00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:34.830
A female has two X chromosomes.

38
00:02:35.590 --> 00:02:41.190
Early on, each cell silences one of them at random, so her cortex is a mosaic.

39
00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:47.170
On the standard interpretation, neurons with the normal X active reach the cortex.

40
00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:52.550
Neurons with the mutant X active stall partway and form a band.

41
00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:57.190
The usual reading is that each neuron follows the X it keeps active.

42
00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:03.570
So ask which step failed: too few neurons made, or neurons made but misplaced.
